WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.458
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.458 --> 00:00:05.720
I help some content streamers
figure out their optimal window

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.440
sizes.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:07.910
Will we fetch the
right solution?

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:09.281
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.281 --> 00:00:11.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:14.870
For the internet video
streaming company Mega Streams,

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.730
the most critical aspect
of their operation

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:19.040
is the sweet spot between
user connection speed,

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.860
cloud hosting performance,
and the window size you

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:24.170
use to buffer and request data.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.660
Their current architecture is
serving video data directly

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.400
from Google Cloud
Storage buckets

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.925
to users on mobile
and desktop devices.

00:00:30.925 --> 00:00:32.299
Over the past
month, they've been

00:00:32.299 --> 00:00:35.420
trying to figure out the right
way to duplicate their content

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.810
and optimize their window sizes
so they can ensure consistent

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:42.050
buffering to their clients
across the US and EU,

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.140
which we can help them figure
out by profiling how the read

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.620
performance adjusts at various
window sizes of download.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.410
To figure this out, I
wrote a very simple script

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:54.980
which creates a regional
bucket, creates a bunch of files

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:56.720
at a bunch of
different file sizes,

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.460
and uploads to that
bucket, and then

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:02.030
downloads each one of those
files 100 times via HTTP

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:02.600
Fetch--

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.739
with caching disabled.

00:01:04.739 --> 00:01:05.780
That's important to know.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:08.840
This graph shows the 50th
percentile for those times.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:10.970
And that, for fetches
under one megabyte,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.490
we can see very consistent
read performance.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.760
But one graph never
tells the complete story.

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:18.979
So, we can switch our
test just slightly

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:20.270
to get a better view of things.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.070
Now, let's take
an 8-megabyte file

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:25.040
and fetch it through
different-sized chunks.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:27.170
This graph helps us see
our read performance

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.580
from another perspective.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.450
As the chunk size decreases,
the overhead per transaction

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.900
increases, thus
making things slower.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.630
Combined, these two graphs
illustrate something important.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:41.330
Now, much like write
throughput, GCS

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:44.990
is extremely strong in terms of
single-stream throughput, which

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:47.240
means that for both
uploads and downloads,

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.550
GCS is at its best
performance when

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:52.010
you're favoring larger
requests-- about one

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.210
megabyte in size.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.010
Otherwise, the
per-transaction overhead

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:58.550
of the requests themselves
will start to slow things down.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.920
For Mega Streams, this was
a massively helpful piece

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:01.580
of data.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.121
They now had the
ability to determine

00:02:03.121 --> 00:02:04.580
what level of
regional redundancy

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.980
they needed for serving
their video content in order

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:10.310
to optimize the 1-megabyte GCS
target to improve buffering

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.180
speeds.

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.290
Data saves the day.

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:12.844
[LAUGHS]

00:02:12.844 --> 00:02:15.260
To learn more about how Mega
Streams improved their upload

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:17.087
performance, check out
the related article

00:02:17.087 --> 00:02:18.420
linked in the description below.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.330
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our "Cloud

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.250
Performance Atlas"
content, because when

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:25.037
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

